{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested for retained-files draft3 at 02a59bf889fa4aa9bf087c0b8bfa76ed495bdde3. Two required findings in the rewritten takeover and cleanup paths.\n\nF1 [P1] Re-admit the replacement upload's byte reservation on takeover (notes/2026-10-21-retained-files.md:303-306).\nThe no-row branch checks and charges the declared size, but the expired-pending branch changes only owner and lease and orders the earlier key deleted. It does not compare the new request's size/media with the earlier declaration or recheck the new byte charge. A reserves digestD with Content-Length1 and never finishes; after expiry B requests that same D with its correct32MiB body. B takes over the one-byte reservation, writes the large object, and Complete makes the charge32MiB without a capacity check. This bypasses both the scope byte reservation and pending-byte limit. An unverified pending declaration is not evidence of D's real size.\n\nDefine takeover as either a refusal of changed metadata or a fresh, atomic admission of the replacement declaration with the old reservation accounted for. Check file maximum, scopeFileBytes, pending bytes and keeper quota before reading/storing B's body; update the row's size/media/charge consistently. Add a witness with an expired small declared reservation and a larger replacement, at a capacity boundary. It must refuse before storage or hold the full valid reservation, with no interval charged only at A's declaration.\n\nF2 [P2] Keep cleanup responsibility until an old bucket write cannot appear later (notes/2026-10-21-retained-files.md:348-351 and :370-378).\nA takeover or expired-row sweep can order deletion of T's key while T's bucket put is still unresolved. Delete can succeed on the absent key, so the order is removed. T's earlier put can then finish and create the object. If the process stops before stale T runs Complete, the row is owned by another transfer or gone, and there is no remaining delete order for T. The assertion that every bucket object has an owner row or delete order is then false. Per-transfer keys protect the newer file, but do not make an absent-key delete final while an earlier write can still complete.\n\nDefine when an abandoned transfer is known unable to write again, and preserve durable cleanup responsibility until then. For example, retain and reconcile a retired-transfer record, or establish and witness guaranteed write quiescence before dropping its order. A lease stops authorization and body reads; it does not by itself prove an outstanding keeper call has completed or been cancelled. Add the order: reserveT, putT outstanding, takeover/sweep, successful absent-key delete, late putT completion, process stop before Complete, recovery. T's object must still be found and deleted without its HTTP callback returning. Record its storage/quota ownership until cleanup is final.\n\nBoth specific findings from cc7151fe are otherwise corrected: stale completions now check owner/state/lease and only clean their own key, and retained_file plus every SQLite operation now carries inc and digest, with witness13 on both keepers. The ordinary tombstone-first redaction path and the corrected slice ordering remain intact. The two sequences above concern new branches introduced by the transfer rewrite. No other required finding remains after the full-note reread.\n\nI read all609primary lines first, then inspected exact primary010ab32b and formal request07770ac2. The primary is effective, fresh, live and not describing a superseded world under planner promiseacc6b225 for request070940da; native prepare assigns it. The cited Main evidence is a physically distinct copy of hash-verified explicit leaves of the frozen prior review. No completed original, helper or root was executed or edited.\n\nThis candidate adds only the32,470B note, SHA256ca9fab4a1cacdb30b60d4fd3c3aaae4c8d01087bf3e19293a94772ba0b981530. All2,621 Main603220b9 paths and modes are unchanged. Whitespace check pas